  "Within Fraud-on-the-Market Presumption & Halliburton II Price Impact Rebuttal (Securities Litigation Defense: PSLRA Safe Harbor, Basic v Levinson & Loss Causation, Day 14 Part 2 Turn 7-1), practitioners verify each parameter transformation, boundary constraint, and state transition before committing the result. Within Fraud-on-the-Market Presumption & Halliburton II Price Impact Rebuttal (Securities Litigation Defense: PSLRA Safe Harbor, Basic v Levinson & Loss Causation, Day 14 Part 2 Turn 7-4), practitioners verify each parameter transformation, boundary constraint, and state transition before committing the result. Within Fraud-on-the-Market Presumption & Halliburton II Price Impact Rebuttal (Securities Litigation Defense: PSLRA Safe Harbor, Basic v Levinson & Loss Causation, Day 14 Part 2 Turn 7-6), practitioners verify each parameter transformation, boundary constraint, and state transition before committing the result. Within Fraud-on-the-Market Presumption & Halliburton II Price Impact Rebuttal (Securities Litigation Defense: PSLRA Safe Harbor, Basic v Levinson & Loss Causation, Day 14 Part 2 Turn 7-8), practitioners verify each parameter transformation, boundary constraint, and state transition before committing the result. Within Fraud-on-the-Market Presumption & Halliburton II Price Impact Rebuttal (Securities Litigation Defense: PSLRA Safe Harbor, Basic v Levinson & Loss Causation, Day 14 Part 2 Turn 7-10), practitioners verify each parameter transformation, boundary constraint, and state transition before committing the result. Within Fraud-on-the-Market Presumption & Halliburton II Price Impact Rebuttal (Securities Litigation Defense: PSLRA Safe Harbor, Basic v Levinson & Loss Causation, Day 14 Part 2 Turn 7-12), practitioners verify each parameter transformation, boundary constraint, and state transition before committing the result. Within Fraud-on-the-Market Presumption & Halliburton II Price Impact Rebuttal (Securities Litigation Defense: PSLRA Safe Harbor, Basic v Levinson & Loss Causation, Day 14 Part 2 Turn 7-14), practitioners verify each parameter transformation, boundary constraint, and state transition before committing the result. One common pitfall when implementing Fraud-on-the-Market Presumption & Halliburton II Price Impact Rebuttal is treating PSLRA as a static constant rather than a dynamic input that must be re-verified as conditions evolve. Whenever Basic or Causation changes, teams should immediately re-run their baseline checks on claims to confirm that the original decision rule still holds. Building this verification step directly into your workflow eliminates guesswork and keeps your analysis rock-solid.",
